# evidence-for: FR-019, AC-023, AC-024, US-006, FR-039, AC-039
# evidence-volatile: \d+\.\d+s
# evidence-timeout: 900
#
# D-168, GRIND cycle 17 (second casting-4 dispatch). survey.py's decorator join skipped a
# quoted span one line at a time, on the assumption that a quoted string cannot cross a
# newline. A triple-quoted one can, and the `)` on its later lines was counted as code:
# the join stopped before methods=["POST"], `whole_call` was wrongly true, and the Flask
# GET default published `GET /exports` for a route serving POST alone. That is the phantom
# endpoint FR-019's join exists to prevent, reached through the one door the cycle-8 fix
# for the same class did not cover.
#
# The open delimiter is now carried out of one line and back into the next. Block [1] is
# the measurement: five trees that differ only in the note the route carries, each run
# against the script at 2333cf1 and against this commit. Block [2] is the red-first row
# the fix comes with. Block [3] is what moved, executable lines only, tokenized rather
# than eyeballed off the diff.
#
# The six-line cap is unchanged and is measured here rather than assumed: a note that
# closes past it leaves the call unread, and the fix changes that case from a published
# GET to no entry at all -- the direction survey.py's own comments say this scan is
# allowed to fail in, because a writer sent to document a phantom finds nothing there.

== [1] the defect. Every tree declares methods=["POST"]; only the note differs ==
   triple-quoted note, ) on its second line  before [('GET', '/exports')]   after [('POST', '/exports')]
   the '''  spelling, same shape             before [('GET', '/exports')]   after [('POST', '/exports')]
   single-quoted note, one line (control)    before [('POST', '/exports')]   after [('POST', '/exports')]
   note closes past the cap, ) inside it     before [('GET', '/exports')]   after []
   note closes past the cap, ) past it too   before []   after []

   before = survey.py at 2333cf1, the commit the defect was measured at.
   after  = the script this commit carries, which is what the gate re-runs.
   GET is the phantom: the verb these routes answer 405 to, published as the
   product's surface. The last two rows are the cap. The join reads six
   continuation lines and neither note ends inside them, so the call is never
   read to its end and no verb can be read off it. Before, the ) on a line the
   join did reach balanced the call and the Flask GET default fired anyway;
   after, nothing inside an open string is counted, the depth never reaches
   zero, and the route is left out rather than published wrong. The fifth row
   carries its ) past the cap, so it was already unpublished before the fix --
   it is the control showing the cap was masking the fourth row, not fixing it.

== [2] the red-first row: RED at 2333cf1, green now ==
   cfafe8e       -> 1 failed, 30 deselected in 0.35s
   3aa48f7       -> 1 failed, 30 deselected in 0.34s
   339a80d       -> 1 failed, 30 deselected in 0.10s
   2333cf1       -> 1 failed, 30 deselected in 0.05s
   this commit   -> 1 passed, 30 deselected in 0.05s

   The row names 2333cf1 because that is where the phantom and its control stand
   together: /reports publishes the PATCH it declares while the other three go
   GET. At 339a80d and 3aa48f7 there is no paren-in-a-string handling at all and
   /reports goes GET too; at cfafe8e there is no decorator joining and nothing is
   published for any of the four routes.

